---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Frieze from "../components/Frieze.astro";

const facts = [
  { term: "Build", detail: "Astro, static output. No server, no database." },
  { term: "Styling", detail: "Tailwind v4, with the tokens shared with lantip.space." },
  { term: "Type", detail: "EB Garamond Variable and Fragment Mono, served from here." },
  { term: "Maps", detail: "Public-domain Dutch East Indies charts, converted to duotone." },
  { term: "Tracking", detail: "None. No analytics, no cookies, no newsletter." },
  { term: "JavaScript", detail: "None shipped. It is HTML and CSS." },
];
---
<Base
  title="Colophon — Azza Sawungrana"
  description="How this site is made: Astro, Tailwind, self-hosted type, public-domain maps, a CV built from one file, and no tracking."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6">
    <header class="mt-10 md:mt-14">
      <p class="stamp-text text-ink-soft">colophon</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-[2rem] leading-[1.12] text-ink-deep md:text-[2.5rem]">
        I built this because I wanted a CV I could keep true.
      </h1>
      <div class="mt-5 max-w-2xl space-y-4 text-[1.06rem]">
        <p>
          The old one was a template I had stopped believing in. It still called me a student at
          Twente, which stopped being accurate in July 2025, and every time I updated it I had to
          remember which of four places held the same sentence.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the first job was to make the CV exist once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <Frieze
      src="/plates/java.webp"
      alt="Oudheidkundige kaart van West-Java: an engraved map of the north coast"
      caption="oudheidkundige kaart van west-java · public domain, via wikimedia commons"
    />

    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-4">
      <p>
        Everything on <a href="/cv">/cv</a>, and both PDFs, comes from a single YAML file. A schema
        checks it when the site builds. If a date is wrong or a field is missing, the build fails
        and tells me which line. That is most of the reason this site exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are two PDFs on purpose. One is designed, on paper, for a person to read. The other
        is plain and white and single column, for the software that many large employers run
        before a person sees anything. A beautiful CV that a parser cannot read is a CV nobody
        reads.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper texture and the ornaments come from{" "}
        <a href="https://lantip.space">lantip.space</a>, which is the other site I keep. Two visual
        languages for one person seemed like a waste of a good afternoon.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Rule />

    <dl class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
        facts.map((f) => (
          <div class="border-t rule-line pt-2">
            <dt class="stamp-text text-ink-soft">{f.term}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 text-[0.98rem] text-ink">{f.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <Rule />

    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-4 pb-10">
      <p>
        The maps are real ones. They come from the Rijksmuseum and the Dutch National Archives by
        way of Wikimedia Commons, all public domain, and a small script in <code>tools/</code>
        turns them into navy on paper. Batavia, the Javanese north coast, the archipelago as it
        was drawn in 1873. I like that the paper under this site is a map of where I am from.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing here is tracked. There is no cookie prompt because there are no cookies, and the
        whole thing is HTML and CSS. It will still work when the tools I used to build it are
        gone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
